WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.958
STEPHANIE WONG:
Today we're talking

00:00:01.958 --> 00:00:05.300
about BigQuery, more BigQuery,
and Java for Cloud Functions.

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.790
I am Stephanie Wong, and
welcome to This Week in Cloud

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:09.560
where we serve you
the lowest latency

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:11.600
news from the safety of home.

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:14.600
Google Cloud Functions is an
event=driven serverless compute

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:17.330
platform that lets you build
and deploy applications

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.960
in a fully-managed environment,
both locally and in the cloud.

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.930
And we've just announced that we
are adding support for Java 11.

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:26.660
This means you can now write
Cloud Functions in Java

00:00:26.660 --> 00:00:28.550
or in your favorite
JVM language,

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:31.010
such as Kotlin,
Groovy, or Scala.

00:00:31.010 --> 00:00:35.340
Support for Java 11 is now
in beta, so try it out.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.910
BigQuery recently turned
10, and to celebrate we're

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:39.910
giving it some
well-deserved attention.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.750
In this birthday
blog post we recap

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:43.880
some of the major
milestones for BigQuery,

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:46.490
from the early days of community
support, as we figured out

00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:48.845
how to apply SQL, to big data.

00:00:48.845 --> 00:00:50.930
A major advantage to
BigQuery is that it

00:00:50.930 --> 00:00:53.010
offers a choice
in pricing models,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.000
so you can do big things on
whatever budget you have.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.040
You can choose between the
entirely free BigQuery sandbox,

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.730
a pay-as-you-go approach
with on-demand pricing,

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.430
or flat-rate pricing
for dedicated resources.

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:06.440
Learn more about
your options and how

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:09.950
to decide which is best
for you in this blog post.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.160
Finally, we've recently started
a new video series, BigQuery

00:01:13.160 --> 00:01:16.580
Spotlight, to help you explore
the ins and outs of this fully

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.080
managed data warehouse.

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:19.730
Each topic is less
than five minutes

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.790
so that you can learn something
new, even in these busy times.

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:25.520
Check it out today, and be sure
to subscribe for notifications

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:27.007
on these future videos.

00:01:27.007 --> 00:01:29.090
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:31.290
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:32.490
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:33.840
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.280
We'll see you in the Cloud.

